  "verbalization": "Dexamethasone is implicated in the development of corneal ulcer through a series of indirect pathways involving corticosteroid-induced inflammation. The evidence suggests that Dexamethasone, through its indication of panuveitis, may contribute to corneal ulceration via the use of related corticosteroids such as Medrysone and Fluorometholone. These corticosteroids are known to modulate inflammatory responses, which can lead to ocular complications, including corneal ulceration. The presence of a final score of 0.5016 in multiple paths indicates a consistent, though not absolute, association between Dexamethasone and corneal ulceration, likely mediated through its effects on inflammatory processes. The mechanisms are not fully elucidated, but the shared pathway of corticosteroid use and the clinical link to panuveitis suggest a plausible mechanistic connection. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in modulating ocular inflammation, which may contribute to the development of corneal ulceration.",
